+# Chrome 149 desktop network capture report
+
+## Binaries
+
+| Platform | Build | Source |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Linux x86_64 | Chrome-for-Testing **149.0.7827.115** | `chrome-for-testing-public/.../linux64/chrome-linux64.zip` |
+| Windows x64 | Chrome-for-Testing **149.0.7827.115** | `chrome-for-testing-public/.../win64/chrome-win64.zip` (downloaded; not executed) |
+| macOS arm64 | Chrome-for-Testing **149.0.7827.115** | `chrome-for-testing-public/.../mac-arm64/chrome-mac-arm64.zip` |
+| Host goldens | Chromium **149.0.7827.114** Nitrous SSLClientSocket | Existing `chromium_149_macos_*_client_hello.hex` |
+
+Exact Nitrous version `149.0.7827.114` was not published on Chrome-for-Testing;
+`.115` is the nearest published 149.0.7827.* desktop build used for independent
+verification.
+
+## Capture method
+
+1. Raw TCP listener accepts the first TLS record and records the ClientHello
+   handshake payload (`tools/capture_client_hello.py` in the capture workspace).
+2. Headless Chrome navigates to `https://<listener>/` with certificate errors
+   ignored so the ClientHello is emitted before the handshake fails.
+3. Linux Chrome ran under Docker `--platform linux/amd64` on an Apple Silicon
+   host (QEMU user emulation).
+4. macOS Chrome ran natively.
+
+## Results
+
+| Capture | SHA-256 (ClientHello body) | Notes |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Linux CFT default | `62b9360fc1bbb3c2a113df1ba4c1169a145a6c2548596e11516ca34f748aa50a` | 1791 bytes |
+| macOS CFT default | `36618a0bfdb2d97bbcb92712297c86a9af8e3e2d955f2a995c53ff4698118a00` | 1762 bytes |
+
+GREASE-normalized structural fields **match** between Linux and macOS CFT:
+
+- Cipher suites
+- Signature algorithms (default profile; no ML-DSA in CFT default)
+- Supported versions
+- Supported groups
+- ALPN (`h2`, `http/1.1`)
+
+Extension **order** differs between connections (and platforms) and is the
+dimension Nitrous steers with `SSL_set_extension_permutation_seed`.
+
+## Product conclusion
+
+Chrome 149 desktop forms a single **network wire cohort** for Device Packs on
+macOS / Windows / Linux claims. Host emission goldens remain the SSLClientSocket
+fixtures used by regression tests. CFT captures are retained here as independent
+cross-OS evidence, not as alternate production template ids.
+
+Windows x64 execution was not available on the capture machine; Windows packs
+reuse the desktop cohort based on the macOS↔Linux structural match and the
+shared Chromium/BoringSSL desktop stack.
